pra: 0168 - emmctest=1 telemetry mode: one CMD23+CMD18 probe per request, always per-sector

DRAFT (not committed; produced by the eMMC speed-work agent). STACKS ON
0003 + 0004. Generated against the reconstructed CI-built tree (pristine
xnu-8796.141.3 + 0001..0165 with pre-skips 0031/0041/0097/0099/0127/0129,
GNU patch -p1 --fuzz=0, index lines stripped). Verify with GNU
`patch -p1 --fuzz=0 --dry-run`, NOT git apply.

PROBLEM: the CMD23+CMD18 question (does SET_BLOCK_COUNT close the stream
on this Arasan+card, or does the 0162-class wedge return through the CMD23
door?) needs per-chunk forensics from a REAL boot. emmcmulti=1 collects it
but risks the boot (latch caps it at 2 strikes - evidence, then per-sector
for the rest of the boot, with the ladder never climbed past the first
failure). There was no way to sample multi behaviour continuously while
guaranteeing multi never stays on.

CHANGES - boot-arg emmctest=1 (default OFF; without it the boot is
byte-identical to the 0004 state):

- On the FIRST whole-sector read of >= 2 sectors of every strategy
  request, strat attempts ONE CMD23+CMD18 chunk at the current ladder
  rung, REGARDLESS of the latch (pra_emmc_multi_ok stays false all boot;
  fail_run untouched). The probe's data is DISCARDED - the request then
  reads the same sectors through the proven per-sector CMD17 path - so
  even a subtly corrupt stream (the 0142 stale-FIFO class) cannot reach
  the filesystem. Zero boot risk by construction.
- The ladder rung still climbs on probe success and resets on probe
  failure, so one boot covers every rung 2..128: if the wedge is
  rung-dependent (a stream that survives 2 sectors but wedges at 64), the
  telemetry will show exactly where.
- Any probe failure runs the escalated pra_emmc_recover() (0004: a/b/c)
  and one rate-limited strat ring flush (0003), then the request
  continues per-sector - the lifeline survives.
- Full per-chunk telemetry, one line: rung (sectors), err, stage
  (0 ok / 1 CMD23 / 2 CMD18 command / 3 block / 4 transfer-complete /
  5 busy-at-entry), last status word, first failing block, recovery
  level recover() reached, elapsed us, running totals
  (chunks/ok/fail). First 32 lines always print (covers the ladder
  climb); afterwards at most one line per 10 s with a suppressed count -
  a wedged probe per request cannot flood the console.
- read_multi records pra_emmc_multi_stage/stage_block at every phase
  exit (cheap, unconditional - also improves the 0003 failure line's
  evidence for future forensics).

WHAT A WEDGE WOULD LOOK LIKE (emmctest=1): "test chunk ... err=EIO
stage=1 status=..." (CMD23 rejected - card does not take SET_BLOCK_COUNT)
or "err=EBUSY stage=3 block=N" mid-drain then "recover(a) STILL BUSY" +
"recover(b) ...", "recover(c) ..." - i.e. the 0162 signature returning
through the CMD23 door, with the status words the 0162 boots lost.
Success shape: "test chunk ... err=0 stage=0" with sectors climbing
2 -> 128 and the request still completing per-sector.

COST: every whole-sector request >= 1 KiB pays one extra chunk attempt
(2 sectors ~ 0.4 ms at PIO rates, more on a failing card) plus - on probe
failure - one bounded recover. The boot gets slower; it never gets dead.
Do not ship this mode with a release image: it is a diagnostics boot.

DEFAULT BOOT (no emmctest arg): byte-identical - the only unconditional
code changes are stage/status bookkeeping inside read_multi's existing
error paths (stores to statics, no prints, no IO).

